📋 1. The Three Requisition Styles
Style	Syntax	Usage	Pros / Cons
1. Whole Toolbox	import math	
math.sqrt(50)


math.pi

Clear & Safe: Never causes naming conflicts, but requires the module. prefix everywhere.
2. Specific Tool	from math import sqrt	sqrt(50)	Direct & Concise: Call it bare (sqrt()). However, math.sqrt won't work anymore.
3. Crib Query	
dir(math)


help(math.sqrt)

—	Inspection Tools: dir() lists all available tools in the box; help() opens the manual.
📦 2. Floor vs. Ceiling Thinking (Shop Operations)
When packing or splitting batches in manufacturing, integer division and standard rounding aren't always enough:

Floor Thinking (// or math.floor): "How many full units can I complete?"

Example: 137÷12=11 full boxes you can completely fill (137 // 12 = 11).

Ceiling Thinking (math.ceil): "How many containers/boxes must I order to hold everything?"

Example: To hold 137 parts when each box holds 12, you need 12 boxes (math.ceil(137 / 12) = 12).

Python
import math

parts = 137
capacity = 12

full_boxes = parts // capacity       # Output: 11  (Boxes completely filled)
boxes_needed = math.ceil(parts / 12) # Output: 12  (Boxes required to ship all parts)
